[Event "Chapter 1: The Fried Liver Trap / Study 1: Black Plays 5...Nxd5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/82cae816-ea22-465e-91da-4b345fcd45af/studies/ce648a16-acce-4e4c-bb4f-3b20c481f0d7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {After 1.e4 e5, White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game! The bishop on c4 immediately eyes the weak f7-pawn (only defended by Black's king).} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {With 3...Nf6, Black is allowing the Fried Liver Attack. This move is tied in popularity with 3...Bc5, but the lower we go down the rating ladder, the more popular 3...Nf6 gets. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5! We generally don't want to move the same piece twice, but in this case, White is threatening a dangerous fork on f7, which isn't defended easily if Black doesn't know what they're doing.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the best move that Black can go for — blocking our bishop on c4. It's also the most popular, happening in 75% of amateur games. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Nxd5 {As Black, most players are simply tempted to capture. 5...Nxd5 is the most popular move here. This allows for an aggressive continuation as White: we capture the pawn on f7, sacrificing the knight!} 6.Nxf7 6...Kxf7 {Black has to recapture since this comes with a double attack. Now that we've lured out Black's king to f7, the bishop's power increases as it pins the knight on d5. From now on, White's plan revolves around opening up the center and adding pressure to d5. We start by giving a check with Qf3, creating a double attack.} 7.Qf3+ 7...Ke6 {35% of games go like this: Black wants to defend their extra piece, but Black's king could find itself in danger. We can start by developing a piece while adding more pressure to the pinned knight.} 8.Nc3 8...Nb4 {8...Nb4 is the second way that Black can go for when trying to deliver a fork on c2. We'll castle here, preparing to get the rook into the action and getting our king into safety before opening up the center.} 9.O-O 9...c6 {Black supports the knight on d5, which is the most popular continuation, occured 80% of the time. It is time to play d4, looking to open up the center and open lines for our minor pieces and the rooks.} 10.d4 10...Qf6 {Nearly 30% of the time, Black offers a queen trade with 10...Qf6. We decline, of course. Trading the queens would only help Black. White's queen can drop back to e2 where it continues to x-ray the king.} 11.Qe2 11...Ke7 {Let's check out the top engine line. 11...Ke7 is played very rarely, 10% of the games went like this. We keep our foot on the gas pedal: the knight jumps to e4, where it is very active.} 12.Ne4 12...Qg6 {Black sidesteps the attack and prepares ...Bf5. c2-c3 supports the pawn on d4 and attacks the b4-knight.} 13.c3 13...Bf5 {Black pressures our e4-knight. We'll support it with f2-f3. In case Black decides to trade, we'll recapture and get the open f-file.} 14.f3 14...Na6 {Black saves the knight.} 15.dxe5 {And by grabbing the pawn on e5 we create an outpost on d6 and open up the d-file for our rook to put pressure on Black's king.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Fried Liver Trap / Study 1: Black Plays 5...Nxd5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/82cae816-ea22-465e-91da-4b345fcd45af/studies/ce648a16-acce-4e4c-bb4f-3b20c481f0d7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {After 1.e4 e5, White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game! The bishop on c4 immediately eyes the weak f7-pawn (only defended by Black's king).} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {With 3...Nf6, Black is allowing the Fried Liver Attack. This move is tied in popularity with 3...Bc5, but the lower we go down the rating ladder, the more popular 3...Nf6 gets. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5! We generally don't want to move the same piece twice, but in this case, White is threatening a dangerous fork on f7, which isn't defended easily if Black doesn't know what they're doing.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the best move that Black can go for — blocking our bishop on c4. It's also the most popular, happening in 75% of amateur games. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Nxd5 {As Black, most players are simply tempted to capture. 5...Nxd5 is the most popular move here. This allows for an aggressive continuation as White: we capture the pawn on f7, sacrificing the knight!} 6.Nxf7 6...Kxf7 {Black has to recapture since this comes with a double attack. Now that we've lured out Black's king to f7, the bishop's power increases as it pins the knight on d5. From now on, White's plan revolves around opening up the center and adding pressure to d5. We start by giving a check with Qf3, creating a double attack.} 7.Qf3+ 7...Ke6 {35% of games go like this: Black wants to defend their extra piece, but Black's king could find itself in danger. We can start by developing a piece while adding more pressure to the pinned knight.} 8.Nc3 8...Nb4 {8...Nb4 is the second way that Black can go for when trying to deliver a fork on c2. We'll castle here, preparing to get the rook into the action and getting our king into safety before opening up the center.} 9.O-O 9...c6 {Black supports the knight on d5, which is the most popular continuation, occured 80% of the time. It is time to play d4, looking to open up the center and open lines for our minor pieces and the rooks.} 10.d4 10...Nxc2 {In 40% of the games, Black grabs the pawn on c2 and attacks our rook. We can completely ignore the material losses and continue our attack with dxe5!, continuing to open up the position. King safety is what decides this game.} 11.dxe5 11...Nxa1 {80% of the time Black went for the material. Now we grab the knight. Black doesn't have enough resources to defend it.} 12.Nxd5 12...cxd5 {Black recaptures. Now, we increase the pressure by pinning the pawn to the queen on d8, while bringing our rook into the game.} 13.Rd1 13...Qa5 {Black moves away from the d-file pin. We play Bxd5+! The king hunt begins.} 14.Bxd5+ 14...Ke7 {Black retreats. Now the queen invades the f7-square with check.} 15.Qf7+ 15...Kd8 {The only legal move. Now there is a checkmate in two moves, starting by bringing our last piece into the game!} 16.Bg5+ 16...Be7 {Black must block with the bishop.} 17.Qxe7# {Checkmate! Going for the rook on a1 was a mistake by Black.} *

