 center.} 9.O-O 9...c6 {Black supports the knight on d5, which is the most popular continuation, occured 80% of the time. It is time to play d4, looking to open up the center and open lines for our minor pieces and the rooks.} 10.d4 10...Qf6 {Nearly 30% of the time, Black offers a queen trade with 10...Qf6. We decline, of course. Trading the queens would only help Black. White's queen can drop back to e2 where it continues to x-ray the king.} 11.Qe2 11...Bd6 {Black gets the dark-squared bishop out. We now add even more pressure to the center, with f4!, also aiming to open the f1-rook.} 12.f4 12...e4 {Black tries to close down the center as much as the can and open an attack on the d4-pawn. We'll capture on e4; the knight on e4 is a very powerful piece anyway.} 13.Nxe4 13...Qxd4+ {Black captures on d4 with check...} 14.Kh1 {...we'll step away to h1, where the king will be safe. Our plan still revolves around adding pressure to Black's pieces that are clustered in the center, with moves like Rd1 and c3, which threatens a fork.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Fried Liver Trap / Study 1: Black Plays 5...Nxd5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/82cae816-ea22-465e-91da-4b345fcd45af/studies/ce648a16-acce-4e4c-bb4f-3b20c481f0d7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {After 1.e4 e5, White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game! The bishop on c4 immediately eyes the weak f7-pawn (only defended by Black's king).} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {With 3...Nf6, Black is allowing the Fried Liver Attack. This move is tied in popularity with 3...Bc5, but the lower we go down the rating ladder, the more popular 3...Nf6 gets. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5! We generally don't want to move the same piece twice, but in this case, White is threatening a dangerous fork on f7, which isn't defended easily if Black doesn't know what they're doing.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the best move that Black can go for — blocking our bishop on c4. It's also the most popular, happening in 75% of amateur games. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Nxd5 {As Black, most players are simply tempted to capture. 5...Nxd5 is the most popular move here. This allows for an aggressive continuation as White: we capture the pawn on f7, sacrificing the knight!} 6.Nxf7 6...Kxf7 {Black has to recapture since this comes with a double attack. Now that we've lured out Black's king to f7, the bishop's power increases as it pins the knight on d5. From now on, White's plan revolves around opening up the center and adding pressure to d5. We start by giving a check with Qf3, creating a double attack.} 7.Qf3+ 7...Qf6 {In nearly 20% of the games Black blocks the check with their queen. We can now grab our piece back with a check.} 8.Bxd5+ 8...Be6 {Black defends with the bishop. We can win yet another pawn by capturing the knight first.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures, and now we recapture with the queen and attack c7.} 10.Qxc6 10...Bd6 {Black defends c7 while developing. It's time for us to start developing our remaining minor pieces as well, starting with Nc3, controlling the center and potentially eyeing the e4 square.} 11.Nc3 11...Rhf8 {Black aims to castle by hand. d3 opens up our dark-squared bishop and add control over the e4-square.} 12.d3 12...Kg8 {Black gets their king into safety.} 13.O-O {White castles here, getting the king into safety and after bringing out the bishop, connecting the rooks. We are up two pawns and a much better pawn structure.} *

