[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 1: The Solid 5...Na5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/214e8dce-71a0-4787-a04d-a8baf2a9c955/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. Unlike 3...Bc5, which is also very popular, this allows the Fried Liver Attack, since this move blocks the black queen's contact with the g5 square. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Na5 {The previous chapter looked at 5...Nxd5. In 40% of the games, Black goes for the top engine move, ...Na5. It is important for Black to deal with our strong light-squared bishop on c4, which is attacking the f7-pawn. Black's f6-knight is also attacking the pawn on d5, so we move the bishop out of danger with a check on b5, trying to hold on to the pawn.} 6.Bb5+ 6...c6 {Nearly 80% of the time, Black goes for this pawn sacrifice on c6. After we accept with dxc6, we'll gain a pawn but have to deal with Black's increased activity.} 7.dxc6 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Here, White has many viable options, but in this course we recommend 8.Bd3. The bishop on d3 nicely controls the e4-square, which functions an outpost for our knight.} 8.Bd3 8...Nd5 {This is the second most popular move at the amateur level, with a 15% occurrence rate, but at the master level, it's the main line. Black has opened up an attack on our g5-knight and has 2 approaches — immediate ...Nf4 after ...Bd6, or castling short. We'll retreat the knight to f3, aiming to pressure the e5-pawn. Ne4?! is too slow, since Black is immediately infiltrating with ...Nf4!} 9.Nf3 9...Bd6 {Black supports the attacked pawn and prepares to castle. By castling, we get the king into safety and prepare to invade the e-file with Re1 as well.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black responds in a similar manner, which happens in most games. Re1 now puts pressure on the e5-pawn.} 11.Re1 11...Re8 {Black defends the pawn with their rook. Our other knight develops to c3 as usual. Note that if Black plays 12...Nxc3, we recapture with the d-pawn, opening up lines for our queen and bishop. We are up a pawn and are generally happy to make central trades.} 12.Nc3 12...f5 {Black expands with ...f5, threatening a fork on e4. White has less space and an extra pawn here, so trading pieces with Nxd5 is a good idea, even though we do give Black an imposing center.} 13.Nxd5 13...cxd5 {Black recaptures the piece. We'll tuck the bishop back to f1, from where it plays a defensive role and can be unleashed later.} 14.Bf1 14...Rb8 {Black occupies the b-file. To counter Black's strong center, we expand with d4 and pose a question to the e5-pawn; do you want to trade or advance?} 15.d4 15...e4 {Trading would only benefit White, so Black decided to advance and take space.} 16.Ne5 {The e5-square becomes a great outpost for our knight — since none of Black's pawns can attack it. A nice plan could be b3 with Bb2 fianchetto coming. If Black decides to capture twice on e5, we would skewer them with Bf4, winning the exchange.} *

