1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. Unlike 3...Bc5, which is also very popular, this allows the Fried Liver Attack, since this move blocks the black queen's contact with the g5 square. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Na5 {The previous chapter looked at 5...Nxd5. In 40% of the games, Black goes for the top engine move, ...Na5. It is important for Black to deal with our strong light-squared bishop on c4, which is attacking the f7-pawn. Black's f6-knight is also attacking the pawn on d5, so we move the bishop out of danger with a check on b5, trying to hold on to the pawn.} 6.Bb5+ 6...c6 {Nearly 80% of the time, Black goes for this pawn sacrifice on c6. After we accept with dxc6, we'll gain a pawn but have to deal with Black's increased activity.} 7.dxc6 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Here, White has many viable options, but in this course we recommend 8.Bd3. The bishop on d3 nicely controls the e4-square, which functions an outpost for our knight.} 8.Bd3 8...Nd5 {This is the second most popular move at the amateur level, with a 15% occurrence rate, but at the master level, it's the main line. Black has opened up an attack on our g5-knight and has 2 approaches — immediate ...Nf4 after ...Bd6, or castling short. We'll retreat the knight to f3, aiming to pressure the e5-pawn. Ne4?! is too slow, since Black is immediately infiltrating with ...Nf4!} 9.Nf3 9...Bd6 {Black supports the attacked pawn and prepares to castle. By castling, we get the king into safety and prepare to invade the e-file with Re1 as well.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black responds in a similar manner, which happens in most games. Re1 now puts pressure on the e5-pawn.} 11.Re1 11...f5 {The immediate ...f5, sacrificing the e5 pawn, is the computer's top choice. 12.Nxe5 leads to a crazy engine line, but we're not playing that. Instead, we recommend 12.Rxe5!? This move has almost never been played.} 12.Rxe5 12...Bxe5 {You'll most probably see this simple recapture. 12...g5!? 13.Nc3 g4 14.Re1 is another crazy yet improbable line.} 13.Nxe5 {Bf1 and d4 are solid ideas. If ...Re8 now, our knight can simply go back to f3. In return for the exchange, we have an extra pawn, a healthy pawn structure, and clear development, and so White has good compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 1: The Solid 5...Na5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/214e8dce-71a0-4787-a04d-a8baf2a9c955/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. Unlike 3...Bc5, which is also very popular, this allows the Fried Liver Attack, since this move blocks the black queen's contact with the g5 square. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Na5 {The previous chapter looked at 5...Nxd5. In 40% of the games, Black goes for the top engine move, ...Na5. It is important for Black to deal with our strong light-squared bishop on c4, which is attacking the f7-pawn. Black's f6-knight is also attacking the pawn on d5, so we move the bishop out of danger with a check on b5, trying to hold on to the pawn.} 6.Bb5+ 6...c6 {Nearly 80% of the time, Black goes for this pawn sacrifice on c6. After we accept with dxc6, we'll gain a pawn but have to deal with Black's increased activity.} 7.dxc6 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Here, White has many viable options, but in this course we recommend 8.Bd3. The bishop on d3 nicely controls the e4-square, which functions an outpost for our knight.} 8.Bd3 8...Nd5 {This is the second most popular move at the amateur level, with a 15% occurrence rate, but at the master level, it's the main line. Black has opened up an attack on our g5-knight and has 2 approaches — immediate ...Nf4 after ...Bd6, or castling short. We'll retreat the knight to f3, aiming to pressure the e5-pawn. Ne4?! is too slow, since Black is immediately infiltrating with ...Nf4!} 9.Nf3 9...Bd6 {Black supports the attacked pawn and prepares to castle. By castling, we get the king into safety and prepare to invade the e-file with Re1 as well.} 10.O-O 10...Nf4 {Black's knight jumps to f4, looking to take our bishop and double our pawns. This is also the main line at the top level. White simply continues developing with 11.Nc3. The bishop has nowhere to go (Be4 runs into ...f5), but ...Nxd3 is not as bad for White as it may seem.} 11.Nc3 11...Nxd3 {Black's idea is to play against the doubled d-pawns in the future, but White is equipped to defend the d-pawn with moves like Qe2. Meanwhile, the pawn on d3 adds support to e4, and White has ideas of playing Re1 (pressuring e5) or Ne4 (blockading the e pawn).} 12.cxd3 12...O-O {Black gets their king into safety.} 13.b3 {With b3, White prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and occupy the e4-square with Ne4 and Re1. Eventually, White may be able to trade the weak d-pawn off with d3-d4. This line was a part of a game between Fabiano Caruana and Hikaru Nakamura.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 1: The Solid 5...Na5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/214e8dce-71a0-4787-a04d-a8baf2a9c955/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. Unlike 3...Bc5, which is also very popular, this allows the Fried Liver Attack, since this move blocks the black queen's contact with the g5 square. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Na5 {The previous chapter looked at 5...Nxd5. In 40% of the games, Black goes for the top engine move, ...Na5. It is important for Black to deal with our strong light-squared bishop on c4, which is attacking the f7-pawn. Black's f6-knight is also attacking the pawn on d5, so we move the bishop out of danger with a check on b5, trying to hold on to the pawn.} 6.Bb5+ 6...c6 {Nearly 80% of the time, Black goes for this pawn sacrifice on c6. After we accept with dxc6, we'll gain a pawn but have to deal with Black's increased activity.} 7.dxc6 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the pawn. Here, White has many viable options, but in this course we recommend 8.Bd3. The bishop on d3 nicely controls the e4-square, which functions an outpost for our knight.} 8.Bd3 8...h6 {Black poses a question to our knight, but this approach is slow. Our knight can return to the strong e4 square, where it stops Black's bishop from developing to c5. The second most popular move at the top level is ...Ng4, to which we recommend the response Nh3, avoiding the complications of 9.Nf3 f5!?} 9.Ne4 9...Nxe4 {Black trades off our strong piece.} 10.Bxe4 10...Bc5 {Black can now develop the bishop to c5.} 11.O-O {We get the king into safety. White can chose between two plans: a) c3 to push b4 and play Bc2 (or deflect the a5 knight and win c6 if Black allows it) b) d3 with Bf3 and Nc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 1: The Solid 5...Na5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/214e8dce-71a0-4787-a04d-a8baf2a9c955/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. Unlike 3...Bc5, which is also very popular, this allows the Fried Liver Attack, since this move blocks the black queen's contact with the g5 square. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, as it comes with a tempo on the c6-knight and might open up the diagonal to the f7-pawn.} 5.exd5 5...Na5 {The previous chapter looked at 5...Nxd5. In 40% of the games, Black goes for the top engine move, ...Na5. It is important for Black to deal with our strong light-squared bishop on c4, which is attacking the f7-pawn. Black's f6-knight is also attacking the pawn on d5, so we move the bishop out of danger with a check on b5, trying to hold on to the pawn.} 6.Bb5+ 6...Bd7 {In 17% of the games, Black blocks the check with the bishop on d7. The key move for us is Qe2, supporting the bishop on b5, so in case Black trades, we'll recapture with check and get our queen to a more active square.} 7.Qe2 7...Bd6 {Black ignores our plan and develops the dark-squared bishop. We'll trade off the light-squared bishops to get rid of the tension between them and prevent Black from keeping them.} 8.Bxd7+ 8...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 9.Nc3 {The queenside knight finds itself on a natural c3-square, from where we defend the d5-pawn and control the e4-square. Moves like d3 and O-O will come, and White will have a very comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nd4 or 5...b5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/8b3bad00-641d-413d-aa22-4692ec415042/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, gaining a tempo on the c6-knight.} 5.exd5 5...Nd4 {Less than 10% of the time, Black choses to jump to the d4-square. We should react immediately to kick it out with c3. If we play a slow move like O-O, Black can win back the pawn on d5 and have a pleasant position.} 6.c3 6...b5 {Instead of retreating the knight, Black counter-attacks our bishop on c4. 7.cxd4?! bxc4 would yield an equal position where Black will eventually corral White's overextended pawns. The best move is the classy Bf1!, retreating the bishop to a safe square. Black still has to deal with the hanging knight, and if that knight moves away from d4 now, we'll be able to capture on b5 with a check.} 7.Bf1 7...Nxd5 {A retreating knight move like ...Nf5 would be passive. Instead, Black goes for ...Nxd5, played in 75% of games. This move also opens up an attack on our knight. We'll grab a piece on d4.} 8.cxd4 8...Qxg5 {Black regains it by capturing on g5. Now that Black's knight is no longer on d4, we can grab the pawn on b5 with a check!} 9.Bxb5+ 9...Kd8 {Black would lose the castling rights anyway (after ...Bd7), so they move the king to d8. It's time to get our king into safety!} 10.O-O 10...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the light-squared bishop. This is a very key moment, as Black is on the verge of mating us with a combination like ...Ne3! and Qxg2#. The only move that gives White an advantage is Qf3, pinning the knight on e4 to the bishop on b7, temporarily immobilizing Black's pieces.} 11.Qf3 11...Rb8 {Black defends the bishop on b7 so it is no longer hanging. The x-ray on our queen is a bit scary, but we should start with dxe5. Black's position is close to being much worse. Their king is unsafe, and d2-d4 will open up a lot of lines for our pieces.} 12.dxe5 12...Ne3 {We allowed this wild move, hitting the rook and piling on g2.} 13.Qh3 {This is necessary, defending g2 and threatening a mate on d7. After 13...Qxg2+ 14.Qxg2 Nxg2, we play 15.d2-d4 with a small but significant advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nd4 or 5...b5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/8b3bad00-641d-413d-aa22-4692ec415042/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, gaining a tempo on the c6-knight.} 5.exd5 5...b5 {5...b5 is also an interesting move that Black could go for, but it isn't played much, only 4% of the games continue like this. The idea is to lure out our bishop to b5 before capturing on d5. Similar idea to the previous line, the bishop waits on f1 before capturing on b5!} 6.Bf1 6...Qxd5 {Black grabs on d5, which happens in about 50% of the games. However, since we have preemptively tucked away our bishop, we can now develop our knight with a tempo on the queen! Notice how the bishop on f1 nicely covers the g2-pawn.} 7.Nc3 7...Qc5 {Black moves the queen.} 8.Bxb5 {Only now do we slingshot back and capture on b5. This position is suitable for moves like d3 to open up the dark-squared bishop and Qf3 to add pressure to the c6-knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nd4 or 5...b5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/8b3bad00-641d-413d-aa22-4692ec415042/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, gaining a tempo on the c6-knight.} 5.exd5 5...b5 {5...b5 is also an interesting move that Black could go for, but it isn't played much, only 4% of the games continue like this. The idea is to lure out our bishop to b5 before capturing on d5. Similar idea to the previous line, the bishop waits on f1 before capturing on b5!} 6.Bf1 6...Nxd5 {Close to 30% of the time, the pawn on d5 is recaptured with the knight, opening an attack onto g5. However, we can capture on b5 *now*, counterattacking the c6-knight. Notice that Black can no longer play ...Qxd5.} 7.Bxb5 7...Bd7 {In more than half of the games, Black goes for ...Bd7, covering the knight on c7 and unpinning it at the same time. We recommend the simple 8.d3, opening up the dark-squared bishop and fights for the e4-square.} 8.d3 8...Be7 {Black adds an attacker to the knight on g5, but suddenly the downside of ...Bd7 appears: the knight on d5 is loose. Here comes a very surprising move: a sacrifice on f7! We lure the king out into the open.} 9.Nxf7 9...Kxf7 {Black must capture the knight, since it came with a fork. Now Qf3+ comes with another double attack!} 10.Qf3+ 10...Ke6 {Black defends the knight on d5. Note that ...Nf6 would lose to Bxc6. We add pressure to the pinned knight with Nc3.} 11.Nc3 11...Nxc3 {Black trades it off. Before recapturing, we can throw in Bc4+!, forcing the king to d6.} 12.Bc4+ 12...Kd6 {If 12...Nd5, 13.Qxd5 would be checkmate.} 13.bxc3 {And after we recapture, Ba3+ and Qd5+ are both hard to cover as Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Fried Liver Top Lines / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nd4 or 5...b5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/8ce06d4f-2ed6-49c1-85d9-3ef909392493/studies/8b3bad00-641d-413d-aa22-4692ec415042/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {This is the only move that interferes with our c4-bishop. We'll gladly take with our pawn, gaining a tempo on the c6-knight.} 5.exd5 5...b5 {5...b5 is also an interesting move that Black could go for, but it isn't played much, only 4% of the games continue like this. The idea is to lure out our bishop to b5 before capturing on d5. Similar idea to the previous line, the bishop waits on f1 before capturing on b5!} 6.Bf1 6...Nxd5 {Close to 30% of the time, the pawn on d5 is recaptured with the knight, opening an attack onto g5. However, we can capture on b5 *now*, counterattacking the c6-knight. Notice that Black can no longer play ...Qxd5.} 7.Bxb5 7...Bb7 {This move is safer as Black but played less than 7...Bd7, around 40% of the games.} 8.d3 {We'll simply push d3, support the knight on g5, and castle on the next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Traxler / Study 1: Black Plays 5...Ke7 (Good) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/e82b4842-2cf1-4155-b121-db4b1406966c/studies/702a5fbd-d639-44f3-bb22-8c231d4b13a2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...Bc5 {In the Traxler Counterattack, Black ignores the threat of Nxf7 and brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the soft f2-pawn. It's the second most popular move played in 13% of the games. The best move here is Bxf7+! This forces Black to choose between ...Ke7 and ...Kf8. We do not recommend 5.Nxf7, where Black gets a massive initiative after Bxf2+!?.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Ke7 {This first study covers the main line with ...Ke7, which has been played in 50% of games. It is a better move than ...Kf8, because one of Black's main sources of counterplay is ...Rf8. It's time to retreat the bishop to d5, where it looks to eventually capture on c6, weakening Black's control over the center.} 6.Bd5 6...Rf8 {This popular move is also the strongest. Black is starting to pile up on the f2-pawn. By castling, we get the king out of the center and support f2 with the rook as well.} 7.O-O 7...d6 {Black opens up the c8-bishop by pushing ...d6. We'll trade off the knight on c6 to prevent any defensive ideas by Black, such as ...Nc6-d4-e6.} 8.Bxc6 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. Now, Nf3! is a very useful move since we interfere with the rook on f8, which will cover our f2-pawn. We also avoid any possible discovery attacks on the unprotected g5-knight.} 9.Nf3 9...Bg4 {Black pins us. Note that ...Nxe4? runs into d2-d4!, and Black is toast once the e-file opens. If White can deal with the knight on e4, they also have ideas of playing Bg5+, skewering the queen.} 10.d3 {Now we simply start to develop our pieces, beginning with d3. We'll challenge Black's dark-squared bishop with Be3 and bring the knight to d2. White is up a clean pawn and Black has lost castling rights, and their attack will run out of steam once we consolidate.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Traxler / Study 1: Black Plays 5...Ke7 (Good) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/e82b4842-2cf1-4155-b121-db4b1406966c/studies/702a5fbd-d639-44f3-bb22-8c231d4b13a2/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...Bc5 {In the Traxler Counterattack, Black ignores the threat of Nxf7 and brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the soft f2-pawn. It's the second most popular move played in 13% of the games. The best move here is Bxf7+! This forces Black to choose between ...Ke7 and ...Kf8. We do not recommend 5.Nxf7, where Black gets a massive initiative after Bxf2+!?.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Ke7 {This first study covers the main line with ...Ke7, which has been played in 50% of games. It is a better move than ...Kf8, because one of Black's main sources of counterplay is ...Rf8. It's time to retreat the bishop to d5, where it looks to eventually capture on c6, weakening Black's control over the center.} 6.Bd5 6...Nxd5 {Black decides to trade off the bishop, but this is hasty. White is practically winning now. We start by recapturing with a tempo on the c6-knight.} 7.exd5 7...Nd4 {Black doesn't want to be passive, so they jump to the d4-square. We kick the knight away again.} 8.c3 8...Nf5 {The knight retreats to f5.} 9.O-O {And after we castle, the main advantage for White is Black's (non-existent) king safety. We also have an extra pawn! The idea is to shred open the center with d4. In some lines, we may even kick out the knight on f5 with g2-g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Traxler / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Kf8 (Bad) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/e82b4842-2cf1-4155-b121-db4b1406966c/studies/a5c75ff5-79e9-4e8d-9688-d0d564ed2fa4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...Bc5 {In the Traxler Counterattack, Black ignores the threat of Nxf7 and brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the soft f2-pawn. As we've already mentioned in the previous study, Bxf7 offers a simple solution to this position.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Kf8 {Black goes for the bad second option, 5...Kf8. It looks safer, but disallows Black's whole plan of bringing the rook to f8 and counter-attacking us on f2. Here, we retreat the bishop to b3, maintaining its strong diagonal. Unlike when Black played 5...Ke7, Bd5 wouldn't be good here, since Black has ...Nxd5 and our knight on g5 is hanging.} 6.Bb3 6...d6 {...d6 aims to cover the light-squares by opening up the bishop on c8. We'll still deliver a fork on f7!} 7.Nf7 7...Bxf2+ {Black again goes for the sacrifice on f2. We recapture, of course!} 8.Kxf2 8...Nxe4+ {Black grabs on e4 with check. The same idea as with 6...h6 applies! Hide the king on g1 and leave room for the queen on f1.} 9.Kg1 9...Qh4 {This is Black's last resource.} 10.Qf1 {It is easily covered with Qf1.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Traxler / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Kf8 (Bad) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/e82b4842-2cf1-4155-b121-db4b1406966c/studies/a5c75ff5-79e9-4e8d-9688-d0d564ed2fa4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...Bc5 {In the Traxler Counterattack, Black ignores the threat of Nxf7 and brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the soft f2-pawn. As we've already mentioned in the previous study, Bxf7 offers a simple solution to this position.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Kf8 {Black goes for the bad second option, 5...Kf8. It looks safer, but disallows Black's whole plan of bringing the rook to f8 and counter-attacking us on f2. Here, we retreat the bishop to b3, maintaining its strong diagonal. Unlike when Black played 5...Ke7, Bd5 wouldn't be good here, since Black has ...Nxd5 and our knight on g5 is hanging.} 6.Bb3 6...h6 {The third most popular move 6...h6 is attacking our knight, which also isn't a great choice by our opponent. We'll deliver the fork on f7! With the king on f8, there is no counterplay down the f-file to worry about.} 7.Nf7 7...Bxf2+ {Black goes for their cheap tricks with ...Bxf2+, but we can just recapture.} 8.Kxf2 8...Nxe4+ {Black captures on e4 with a check. The king is safe on g1 and we don't mind that the rook on h1 is closed, since we are winning ample material to make up for that. We also want to leave the f1 square available for our queen!} 9.Kg1 9...Qh4 {Black threatens a checkmate, but it's defended easily with Qf1!, lining our queen up with Black's king.} 10.Qf1 10...Ke7 {Black gets out of any discoveries.} 11.Nxh8 {And we just capture the rook on h8. Nobody is checkmating us anytime soon, meanwhile, we're ahead 7 points of material!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Traxler / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Kf8 (Bad) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/e82b4842-2cf1-4155-b121-db4b1406966c/studies/a5c75ff5-79e9-4e8d-9688-d0d564ed2fa4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...Bc5 {In the Traxler Counterattack, Black ignores the threat of Nxf7 and brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the soft f2-pawn. As we've already mentioned in the previous study, Bxf7 offers a simple solution to this position.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Kf8 {Black goes for the bad second option, 5...Kf8. It looks safer, but disallows Black's whole plan of bringing the rook to f8 and counter-attacking us on f2. Here, we retreat the bishop to b3, maintaining its strong diagonal. Unlike when Black played 5...Ke7, Bd5 wouldn't be good here, since Black has ...Nxd5 and our knight on g5 is hanging.} 6.Bb3 6...d5 {Black tries to play against our light-squared bishop by blocking its view. This move is played in 30% of the games. We capture with our pawn, keeping the position open.} 7.exd5 7...Nxd5 {Black grabs it back, opening up an attack onto our knight on g5. Now we bring the queen into the action and threaten a checkmate on f7!} 8.Qh5 8...g6 {Black blocks our checkmate threat, but this opens a new diagonal to the king. The queen can now invade with Qh6+!} 9.Qh6+ 9...Ke8 {Black moves the king to f8, not allowing other checks and not getting into the eye of our b3-bishop.} 10.O-O {After castling, White is doing great here. Moves like Nc3 and d4 aim to open up the center against Black's uncastled king. Re1 will be destructive as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Traxler / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Kf8 (Bad) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6eece124-30ed-4053-8e4d-09c558213eb4/chapters/e82b4842-2cf1-4155-b121-db4b1406966c/studies/a5c75ff5-79e9-4e8d-9688-d0d564ed2fa4/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, we start with 1.e4 e5. White will now develop their kingside knight and pressure the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black also develops a knight while defending their pawn. Now, 3.Bc4 is the signature move that signifies the Italian Game!} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black plays 3...Nf6, attacking the e4 pawn. This allows the Fried Liver Attack. To enter the Fried Liver, we play 4.Ng5!, attacking f7, which is not easy to defend.} 4.Ng5 4...Bc5 {In the Traxler Counterattack, Black ignores the threat of Nxf7 and brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the soft f2-pawn. As we've already mentioned in the previous study, Bxf7 offers a simple solution to this position.} 5.Bxf7+ 5...Kf8 {Black goes for the bad second option, 5...Kf8. It looks safer, but disallows Black's whole plan of bringing the rook to f8 and counter-attacking us on f2. Here, we retreat the bishop to b3, maintaining its strong diagonal. Unlike when Black played 5...Ke7, Bd5 wouldn't be good here, since Black has ...Nxd5 and our knight on g5 is hanging.} 6.Bb3 6...Bxf2+ {This move is somehow the second most popular move, occurring in almost 20% of the games. Let's just say that it's not a good move. We can just grab the bishop without any worry.} 7.Kxf2 7...Nxe4+ {Black thinks that they'll regain the piece...} 8.Nxe4 {...but we can just capture on e4 with the knight and go for Rf1+ and Kg1 to get the king into safety. Black has no counterplay here. Qh4+ is met with Ng3.} *